{%- comment -%}
  GOD SQUAD — Product page
  Phase 8. The first template in this theme that can take money.

  Sources of truth:
  - PHASE-2-DESIGN-SYSTEM.md §27 (the five ecommerce questions), §12 (forms and
    the quantity selector), §10 (buttons), §14.6 (loading strategy), §26.2
    (measure), §29.6 (the settings budget).
  - PHASE-1-WEBSITE-AUDIT.md ECOM-01, ECOM-02, ECOM-04, PROD-04, DEBT-09.

  SHOPIFY IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH.
  Nothing on this page is invented. There is no hard-coded product, price,
  variant, size, colour, stock figure, discount, review, rating, material,
  shipping claim or availability string anywhere in this file. Every value is
  read from the product object, and every price goes through the money filter
  against the store's own format — which is the defect Phase 1 PROD-04 and
  TECHNICAL-4 recorded, where the prototype built prices by string
  concatenation and its currency prop changed the symbol without changing the
  number.

  THE FORM IS SHOPIFY'S OWN.
  {% form 'product', product %} generates the method, the action, the
  form_type and utf8 inputs and the product-id input. It does NOT generate the
  variant input — product-id is not id and adds nothing to a cart — so the
  theme supplies <input name="id"> itself. With JavaScript off this form still
  posts to /cart/add and Shopify redirects to the cart page, which is why
  templates/cart.json exists in this phase.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE.
  No reviews, no ratings, no star row, no stock counter, no "only 3 left", no
  invented materials or care instructions, no country of origin, no shipping
  estimate, no recommendations, no wishlist, no search. Some are later phases;
  the rest are business facts nobody has supplied. variant.inventory_quantity
  is public on the storefront and is deliberately never rendered: it leaks
  either stock levels or lifetime sales, and Phase 1 recorded any inventory
  display as BUSINESS INFORMATION REQUIRED.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign current_variant = product.selected_or_first_available_variant

  comment
    Availability comes from the variant and the product, never from whether a
    variant resolved. selected_or_first_available_variant returns the FIRST
    variant when every variant is sold out, so a nil check always passes and
    would report a sold-out product as buyable.
  endcomment
  assign can_buy = false
  if current_variant != blank and current_variant.available
    assign can_buy = true
  endif

  assign on_sale = false
  if current_variant.compare_at_price > current_variant.price
    assign on_sale = true
  endif

  assign surface_class = 'surface-light'
  if section.settings.surface == 'dark'
    assign surface_class = 'surface-dark'
  endif

  assign classes = 'main-product '
  assign classes = classes | append: surface_class
  assign classes = classes | append: ' main-product--media-' | append: section.settings.media_layout
  if section.settings.sticky_info
    assign classes = classes | append: ' main-product--sticky-info'
  endif

  assign form_id = 'ProductForm-' | append: section.id
  assign qty_id = 'Quantity-' | append: section.id

  comment
    The quantity rule is Shopify's, not the theme's. A merchant who has set a
    minimum, maximum or increment on the variant gets it honoured; a merchant
    who has not gets the plain floor of one. Nothing here invents a ceiling.
  endcomment
  comment
    The threshold the brand owner set: 3. 0 disables the line entirely, and the
    setting shipped at 0 until that decision was made — a threshold nobody has
    chosen is a claim nobody has made.
  endcomment
  assign low_stock_at = section.settings.low_stock_threshold | default: 0
  comment
    One line, and it has to be: inside a liquid tag every LINE is its own
    statement, so a condition split across lines is not a condition.
  endcomment
  assign show_low_stock = false
  if low_stock_at > 0 and current_variant.inventory_management == 'shopify' and current_variant.inventory_policy == 'deny' and current_variant.available and current_variant.inventory_quantity > 0 and current_variant.inventory_quantity <= low_stock_at
    assign show_low_stock = true
  endif

  assign qty_min = 1
  assign qty_step = 1
  assign qty_max = blank
  if current_variant.quantity_rule != blank
    assign qty_min = current_variant.quantity_rule.min | default: 1
    assign qty_step = current_variant.quantity_rule.increment | default: 1
    assign qty_max = current_variant.quantity_rule.max
  endif

  comment
    The media slot, computed and then MEASURED. The gallery is the 1.6fr track
    of --split-60-40 from --bp-lg up, inside a container capped at the theme's
    own content width, and the full viewport below it.

    Three regions, because the buying column has a 22rem floor and that floor
    changes which track absorbs the remainder:

      V < 768           the full viewport less the 24px gutters
      768 <= V < 1024   the full viewport less the 32px gutters
      1024 <= V < 1106  the buying column is pinned at its 352px floor, so the
                        media takes everything else: 100vw - 96 gutters
                        - 32 gap - 352
      1106 <= V < C     neither track is clamped: (100vw - 128) x 1.6/2.5
      V >= C            the container stops growing: (C - 128) x 1.6/2.5

    1106 is where the floor stops binding: 128 + 352 / 0.36. Every one of these
    was checked against the rendered box at ten widths — the first version of
    this declared 573px where the element measured 544, and 901px where it
    measured 840, both because it forgot that max-width includes the padding
    under border-box. Phase 6 measured what an under-declared sizes costs.
  endcomment
  assign container = settings.container_width | default: 1440
  comment
    Integer division floors, so the cap would be declared one pixel under
    the rendered box. One pixel changes no candidate, but under-declaring
    is the direction that costs quality, so it rounds up.
  endcomment
  assign media_cap = container | minus: 128 | times: 64 | divided_by: 100 | plus: 1
  capture media_sizes
    echo '(min-width: '
    echo container
    echo 'px) '
    echo media_cap
    echo 'px, (min-width: 1106px) calc((100vw - 128px) * 0.64), (min-width: 1024px) calc(100vw - 480px), (min-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 64px), calc(100vw - 48px)'
  endcapture
-%}

{{ 'section-main-product.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
<script src="{{ 'product.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>

<div
  class="{{ classes }}"
  data-main-product
  data-section-id="{{ section.id }}"
  data-url="{{ product.url }}"
  data-string-unavailable="{{ 'products.variants.unavailable' | t | escape }}"
  {{ section.shopify_attributes }}
>
  <div class="main-product__inner container">
    <div class="main-product__media">
      {% render 'product-media-gallery',
        product: product,
        section_id: section.id,
        layout: section.settings.media_layout,
        sizes: media_sizes
      %}
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      NO tabindex, role OR aria-label HERE ANY MORE. All three existed for one
      reason: the stylesheet made this column a scroll container, and Safari and
      Chrome before 127 leave scroll containers out of the tab order, so a
      keyboard-only customer could not reach what sat below its fold. The
      description moved out, the column no longer overflows, the inner scroll is
      gone -- and with it the reason for any of them. Left in place they would
      be a tab stop on a plain <div> that does nothing, and a group whose label
      is announced for no reason.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div
      class="main-product__info"
    >
      {%- if section.settings.show_vendor and product.vendor != blank -%}
        <p class="main-product__vendor">{{ product.vendor }}</p>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        Low stock. Text only, no colour-coded pill and no figure — Phase 2
        section 27.3 puts availability in words, and a number here would be the
        inventory disclosure the theme has refused since Phase 8.

        aria-live is deliberately absent: this line changes as a side effect of
        choosing a variant, and the variant change is already announced. A second
        live region would make one action speak twice.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <p
        class="main-product__low-stock"
        data-low-stock
        {% unless show_low_stock %}hidden{% endunless %}
      >{{ 'products.inventory.low_stock' | t }}</p>

      {%- comment -%}
        The page's one h1. Phase 1 HTML-03 recorded the prototype's product
        names as <div>s; here the product's own title is the document's
        heading, which is also what the SEO section of the brief asks for.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <h1 class="main-product__title">{{ product.title }}</h1>

      {%- comment -%}
        Price. Phase 2 §27.2: full contrast, never muted and never gold, with
        the compare-at beside it in the muted token and a line-through. Both
        shapes are always in the DOM and the sale parts are toggled with the
        hidden attribute, so changing variant swaps text rather than rebuilding
        markup — and hidden takes them out of the accessibility tree as well as
        off the screen, which display:none on a wrapper would not have done for
        the visually hidden labels inside.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <p class="main-product__price price" data-price>
        <span class="visually-hidden" data-price-label-sale {% unless on_sale %}hidden{% endunless %}>
          {{- 'products.price.sale_price' | t -}}
        </span>
        <span class="price__current" data-price-current>{{ current_variant.price | money }}</span>
        <span class="price__compare-wrap" data-price-compare-wrap {% unless on_sale %}hidden{% endunless %}>
          <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'products.price.regular_price' | t }}</span>
          <s class="price__compare" data-price-compare>{{ current_variant.compare_at_price | money }}</s>
        </span>
        {%- comment -%}
          Unit price, for anything sold by weight or volume. Rendered only when
          Shopify carries the measurement; it is never constructed here.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          The unit price follows the variant like every other price on this
          page. It was rendered once from the variant the page opened on, with
          no hook and no field in the variant table, so it could not update —
          and a per-litre price left over from a different size is worse than
          none.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if current_variant.unit_price_measurement != blank -%}
          <span class="price__unit">
            {{ current_variant.unit_price | money }}
            <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
            <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'products.price.per' | t }}</span>
            {% if current_variant.unit_price_measurement.reference_value != 1 %}
              {{- current_variant.unit_price_measurement.reference_value -}}
            {% endif %}
            {{ current_variant.unit_price_measurement.reference_unit }}
          </span>
        {%- endif -%}
      </p>

      {%- comment -%}
        THE FORM. Everything from here to endform is one Shopify product form.
        The class string repeats shopify-product-form because passing class:
        replaces the tag's own default rather than adding to it.

        The variant picker is INSIDE it. It was outside, which meant its radios
        were not associated with the form at all — structurally wrong, even
        though the value that is actually submitted is the hidden input below.
        See §14 of the phase document for what the picker does and does not do
        with scripting off.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- form 'product',
        product,
        id: form_id,
        class: 'shopify-product-form main-product__form',
        data-product-form: 'true'
      -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          NO novalidate. It was here because Dawn has it, and it switched off
          exactly the browser constraint validation the quantity selector
          documents as its own defence: with scripting off, min="1" is the only
          thing standing between a typed -3 and a POST to /cart/add. The script
          still clamps the value and still shows its own themed error, so
          nothing is lost by letting the browser do its job as well.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          The variant. Disabled when it cannot be bought, so a form submitted
          with JavaScript off cannot post a sold-out variant; the script
          re-enables it when the customer picks one that is in stock.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <input
          type="hidden"
          name="id"
          value="{{ current_variant.id }}"
          data-variant-input
          {% unless can_buy %}disabled{% endunless %}
        >

        {% render 'product-variant-picker', product: product, section_id: section.id %}

        {%- if section.settings.show_quantity -%}
          <div class="main-product__quantity">
            <p class="main-product__quantity-label" id="QuantityLabel-{{ section.id }}">
              {{ 'products.quantity.label' | t }}
            </p>
            {% render 'quantity-selector',
              id: qty_id,
              name: 'quantity',
              value: qty_min,
              min: qty_min,
              max: qty_max,
              step: qty_step,
              describe: product.title
            %}
          </div>
        {%- else -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            The quantity still has to be SUBMITTED when its control is hidden.

            This is the only quantity input in the form, so without this branch
            turning the setting off submitted no quantity at all. Shopify then
            defaults to 1 — which is correct for most products and wrong for any
            variant whose quantity rule sets a minimum above 1, because the add
            is rejected with a 422 and the customer sees a failure they cannot
            act on. Phase 8 publishes quantity_rule in the variant table, so the
            theme supports those products; a presentation setting must not be
            able to break them.

            assets/product.js keeps this in step on a variant change by the same
            [data-quantity-input] hook the visible control uses.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <input
            type="hidden"
            name="quantity"
            value="{{ qty_min }}"
            data-quantity-input
          >
        {%- endif -%}

        <div class="main-product__actions">
          {%- comment -%}
            One primary add-to-cart, always present, never behind a hover
            reveal (Phase 2 §27.5). The disabled attribute rather than
            aria-disabled is deliberate here and departs from §10.3: that
            section asks for aria-disabled so a disabled control stays
            reachable and its REASON can be read, but here the reason is the
            control's own label — it reads "Sold out" — so nothing is put out
            of reach. A submit button with aria-disabled would still submit on
            Enter, and with JavaScript off it would post an unavailable variant
            to Shopify and land the customer on an error.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <button
            type="submit"
            name="add"
            class="button button--primary button--full main-product__submit"
            data-add-to-cart
            data-label-idle="{{ 'products.form.add_to_cart' | t | escape }}"
            data-label-busy="{{ 'products.form.adding' | t | escape }}"
            data-label-sold-out="{{ 'products.form.sold_out' | t | escape }}"
            {%- comment -%}
              A third label, for a combination that has no variant at all. Not
              the same fact as sold out: sold out means it existed and ran out,
              which invites the customer to wait for it.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            data-label-unavailable="{{ 'products.variants.unavailable' | t | escape }}"
            {% unless can_buy %}disabled{% endunless %}
          >
            <span data-add-to-cart-label>
              {%- if can_buy -%}
                {{ 'products.form.add_to_cart' | t }}
              {%- else -%}
                {{ 'products.form.sold_out' | t }}
              {%- endif -%}
            </span>
          </button>

          {%- if section.settings.show_accelerated_checkout -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              Shopify's own accelerated checkout. The filter emits its own
              container and its own data-shopify attribute, so it is not
              wrapped here — a second wrapper is what makes Shopify's script
              bind twice. Its colours, type and label live in a closed shadow
              DOM and cannot be brought into the God Squad palette; the five
              documented custom properties are the only permitted adjustment
              and the stylesheet sets two of them so the control's height and
              radius match the primary button above it.

              There is no Liquid guard that says whether it will render
              anything. Whether accelerated checkout is enabled at all, and for
              which payment methods, is BUSINESS INFORMATION REQUIRED — so this
              is a setting, and the block it sits in collapses when empty.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {{ form | payment_button }}
          {%- endif -%}
        </div>

        {%- comment -%}
          The form's own error region. Populated only from Shopify's response
          description, never from a raw exception, and empty until something
          fails. assertive because the customer has just pressed a button and
          is waiting on its result.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div
          class="main-product__error"
          data-product-error
          role="alert"
          hidden
        ></div>

        {%- comment -%}
          Phase 14. The confirmation, for the case where nothing else confirms.

          When the cart drawer opens after an add, the drawer IS the feedback —
          it shows the customer exactly what the cart now holds. But the drawer
          is optional twice over: a merchant can set the cart style to "Cart
          page", and can switch auto-open off. In both of those the only
          sighted response to pressing Add to cart was the header count
          changing by one, in the far corner of the screen, which is not a
          confirmation a customer can be expected to notice.

          role="status" rather than assertive: the customer got what they
          asked for, so this waits its turn instead of interrupting. That also
          makes it self-announcing, which is why assets/cart.js does NOT also
          write to the live region when this element is present — a success
          said twice is worse than a success said once.

          The View cart link is a link to a page that exists, wearing the button
          system's secondary rank — the same treatment the drawer already gives
          this same action (cart-drawer.liquid:180). It is the one thing a
          customer wants next if the addition was not what they meant, and it is
          not an interruption because nothing moved focus.

          It ships hidden, and no script is needed to keep it that way: with
          scripting off the form posts natively and Shopify redirects to the
          cart page, which is a stronger confirmation than any of this.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div
          class="main-product__success"
          data-product-success
          role="status"
          hidden
        >
          <span data-product-success-text></span>
          {%- comment -%}
            `main-product__success-link` stays the FIRST class and `class` stays
            before `href`: qa/phase14/cartdoc.py:253 matches
            `main-product__success-link[^>]*href="/cart"` and qa/phase18/focus.py
            labels rows with className.split(' ')[0]. Both still read correctly
            written this way.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <a class="main-product__success-link button button--secondary" href="{{ routes.cart_url }}">
            {{ 'cart.view_cart' | t }}
          </a>
        </div>
      {%- endform -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        Additional information. Only fields the product actually carries, and
        only the ones the merchant has switched on. An empty field is not
        rendered as an empty row.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- liquid
        comment
          The row is rendered if ANY variant carries a SKU, not only the one the
          page opened on. Gated on current_variant.sku the markup was absent
          whenever the first variant had none — and assets/product.js updates the
          SKU by querying [data-variant-sku], so a hook that was never rendered
          could never be filled: the SKU could not appear for any later variant.

          It is hidden rather than emptied when the selected variant has no SKU,
          because a <dt>SKU</dt> standing over an empty <dd> is a labelled row
          that says nothing.
        endcomment
        assign show_sku = false
        if section.settings.show_sku
          for v in product.variants
            if v.sku != blank
              assign show_sku = true
              break
            endif
          endfor
        endif
        assign show_type = false
        if section.settings.show_product_type and product.type != blank
          assign show_type = true
        endif
      -%}
      {%- if show_sku or show_type -%}
        <dl class="main-product__meta">
          {%- if show_type -%}
            <div class="main-product__meta-row">
              <dt>{{ 'products.meta.type' | t }}</dt>
              <dd>{{ product.type }}</dd>
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}
          {%- if show_sku -%}
            <div
              class="main-product__meta-row"
              data-variant-sku-row
              {% if current_variant.sku == blank %}hidden{% endif %}
            >
              <dt>{{ 'products.meta.sku' | t }}</dt>
              <dd data-variant-sku>{{ current_variant.sku }}</dd>
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}
        </dl>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      THE DESCRIPTION SITS OUTSIDE THE BUYING COLUMN, AND THAT IS WHAT REMOVED
      THE COLUMN'S SECOND SCROLLBAR.

      The column is sticky, so it was capped at one viewport with its own
      overflow-y to keep its lower controls reachable. Measured, the cap was the
      problem rather than the cure: the column's content came to 794px against a
      714px cap at 1440x900, and against 614px at 1280x800 -- a scrollbar inside
      a scrollbar on any ordinary laptop.

      The overflow was never the purchase controls. Title 84 + price 26 + form
      327 is 437px and fits every viewport with room to spare; the description
      alone was 286px. Out here the sticky column is short enough to hold itself
      on screen, so the cap, the inner scroll, and the tabindex that existed only
      to make that scroll container reachable all come out rather than being
      tuned.

      It spans both tracks and keeps its own measure: body copy set to the full
      width of a 1440 page is unreadable however much room there is.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="main-product__story">
      {%- if product.description != blank -%}
        {%- if section.settings.description_layout == 'collapsible' -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            A native <details>. No script, no ARIA to get wrong, and it is
            keyboard-operable and announced correctly by default. The purchase
            controls above are never inside it: the brief's rule is that
            essential purchase information is not hidden, and a description is
            not purchase information.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <details class="main-product__details" open>
            <summary class="main-product__details-summary">
              <span>{{ 'products.description' | t }}</span>
              {% render 'icon-chevron' %}
            </summary>
            <div class="main-product__description rte">{{ product.description }}</div>
          </details>
        {%- else -%}
          <div class="main-product__description rte">{{ product.description }}</div>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    The variant table the picker's script resolves combinations against.

    It is written here rather than using {{ product.variants | json }} for two
    reasons. The whole-object dump publishes inventory_quantity and
    inventory_management into the page source, which is the stock-level leak
    this file refuses to make elsewhere; and it is several times larger than
    the six fields the picker uses. Prices are formatted by Liquid because
    money formatting belongs to the store's own format and cannot be done in
    the browser.

    product.variants truncates at 250. A God Squad product reaching that is not
    a realistic case for apparel, and it is recorded as a known limitation
    rather than papered over.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <script type="application/json" data-variant-data>
    [
      {%- for v in product.variants -%}
        {
          "id": {{ v.id | json }},
          "available": {{ v.available | json }},
          "options": {{ v.options | json }},
          "price": {{ v.price | money | json }},
          "compare_at_price": {% if v.compare_at_price > v.price %}{{ v.compare_at_price | money | json }}{% else %}null{% endif %},
          "media_id": {% if v.featured_media != blank %}{{ v.featured_media.id | json }}{% else %}null{% endif %},
          "sku": {{ v.sku | json }},
          {%- comment -%}
            A boolean, never the figure. The threshold comparison happens here so
            inventory_quantity stays server-side: publishing it would leak either
            stock levels or lifetime sales, which Phase 8 refused and Phase 1
            recorded as BUSINESS INFORMATION REQUIRED.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          "low_stock": {% if low_stock_at > 0 and v.inventory_management == 'shopify' and v.inventory_policy == 'deny' and v.available and v.inventory_quantity > 0 and v.inventory_quantity <= low_stock_at %}true{% else %}false{% endif %},
          "quantity_rule": {% if v.quantity_rule != blank %}{ "min": {{ v.quantity_rule.min | json }}, "max": {{ v.quantity_rule.max | json }}, "increment": {{ v.quantity_rule.increment | json }} }{% else %}null{% endif %}
        }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
      {%- endfor -%}
    ]
  </script>

  {%- comment -%}
    Product structured data, emitted exactly once on the page. Shopify injects
    none of its own, so the theme owns it — and it is produced by Shopify's
    structured_data filter rather than hand-built, which is what keeps every
    price, currency, availability URL and per-variant identifier derived from
    real product data. Nothing here can invent a rating or a review count,
    because the filter does not emit them and no review data exists.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <script type="application/ld+json">
    {{ product | structured_data }}
  </script>
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Product information",
  "tag": "section",
  "class": "main-product-section",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Media"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "media_layout",
      "label": "Media layout",
      "info": "Stacked runs every image down the column and needs no script — the editorial default. Carousel shows one at a time with a thumbnail rail. Every image is reachable either way.",
      "options": [
        {
          "value": "stacked",
          "label": "Stacked"
        },
        {
          "value": "carousel",
          "label": "Carousel"
        }
      ],
      "default": "stacked"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Image shape is set once for the whole catalogue in Theme settings > Products, not here. A ratio that differs between two rows of the same shop is a bug rather than a choice."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Buying"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_quantity",
      "label": "Show the quantity selector",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_accelerated_checkout",
      "label": "Show accelerated checkout",
      "default": true,
      "info": "Shopify's own Buy it now button. It only appears when accelerated checkout is enabled for the store, and its colours and typeface are set by Shopify and cannot be themed."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Details"
    },
    {
